---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TechniqueCode from '../../../components/patterns/TechniqueCode.astro';
import CodeFromFile from '../../../components/CodeFromFile.astro';
import {machines,type Machine} from '../../../data/pattern-code';
import TechniqueDirectory from '../../../components/patterns/TechniqueDirectory.astro';
import TechniqueExperiment from '../../../components/patterns/TechniqueExperiment.astro';
import {techniques,prototypeRoot} from '../../../data/pattern-techniques';
import {resolveTechniques} from '../../../lib/pattern-techniques';
import {getCollection} from 'astro:content';
import '../../../styles/pattern-library.css';
import '../../../styles/pattern-prototype.css';
export function getStaticPaths(){
 if(import.meta.env.PROD&&process.env.PATTERN_PROTOTYPE!=='1')return [];
 return [{params:{technique:undefined},props:{slug:null,machine:'commodore-64'}},...techniques.flatMap(t=>[
  {params:{technique:t.slug},props:{slug:t.slug,machine:'commodore-64'}},
  ...(t.slug==='choose-a-target'?[]:machines.map(m=>({params:{technique:`${t.slug}/${m.id}`},props:{slug:t.slug,machine:m.id}})))
 ])];
}
const {slug,machine='commodore-64'}=Astro.props as {slug:string|null;machine:Machine};
const resolved=resolveTechniques(await getCollection('patterns'));
const technique=slug?resolved.find(t=>t.slug===slug):undefined;
if(slug&&!technique)throw Error('Unknown prototype technique');
const machineNames:Record<string,string>={'sinclair-zx-spectrum':'ZX Spectrum','commodore-64':'Commodore 64','commodore-amiga':'Amiga','nintendo-nes':'NES','cross-platform':'Shared technique'};
const breadcrumbs=[{label:'Home',href:'/'},{label:'Pattern Library',href:'/patterns/'},{label:'Prototype',...(technique?{href:prototypeRoot}:{})},...(technique?[{label:technique.title}]:[])];
---
<Layout title={`${technique?.title??'Connected techniques'} | Pattern Library prototype`} description={technique?.summary??'Four techniques, from reading a control to choosing a pursuer’s next turn.'} masthead={false} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} noindex>
 <div class="pattern-library technique-prototype" data-pagefind-ignore="all">
 {!technique?<>
  <header class="pattern-opening"><h1>Code you can<br/>build on.</h1><div><p>Pick your machine. Take a routine. Make it yours.</p><p>Read the code, see what it needs, and fit it into your own update loop. Start with input, connect it to a sound, then explore a small opponent decision.</p><p class="prototype-note">Prototype for review · four techniques. The <a href="/patterns/">current Pattern Library</a> keeps its existing pages and addresses.</p></div></header>

  <section class="code-start" aria-labelledby="code-start-title"><div><h2 id="code-start-title">One press. One note.</h2><p>This C64 reader stores the held controls and finds new presses. The same update can move a player from the held bits and trigger a note from the new ones.</p><p><a href={`${prototypeRoot}read-input/`}>Read the C64 routine and calling instructions</a> · <a href={`${prototypeRoot}read-input/sinclair-zx-spectrum/`}>ZX Spectrum</a> · <a href={`${prototypeRoot}read-input/commodore-amiga/`}>Amiga</a> · <a href={`${prototypeRoot}read-input/nintendo-nes/`}>NES</a></p></div><div><CodeFromFile src="commodore-64/patterns/assembly/framework/input-to-sound/demo.asm" from="update:" to="keep_note:" lang="6502"/></div></section>
  <section class="technique-chain" aria-labelledby="chain-title"><div><h2 id="chain-title">Three jobs, one response.</h2><p>A machine reader tells us what is held. A comparison finds the new press. A sound driver makes the response.</p></div><ol>{resolved.slice(0,3).map((t,i)=><li><span>{['Sample the controls','Find the new press','Trigger the response'][i]}</span><a href={`${prototypeRoot}${t.slug}/`}>{t.title}</a></li>)}</ol></section>
  <TechniqueDirectory/>
  <details class="supporting-experiment"><summary>Supporting experiment: trace held controls and new presses</summary><TechniqueExperiment kind="input"/></details>
  <footer class="pattern-next"><h2>Connections, without a compulsory course.</h2><p>You can use a joystick reader on its own. You can follow it to press detection. The <a href={`${prototypeRoot}choose-a-target/`}>pursuit experiment</a> makes another separation visible: where to aim, and how to choose a turn.</p><p><a href="/patterns/">Browse all existing patterns</a> · <a href="/vault/studies/pac-man/">Read the Pac-Man study</a></p></footer>
 </>:<>
  <header class="pattern-reading-header"><h1>{technique.title}</h1><p class="pattern-summary">{technique.task}</p><p>{technique.summary}</p><p class="prototype-note">Technique page prototype · <a href={prototypeRoot}>Back to the four techniques</a></p></header>

  <nav class="machine-choices" aria-label="Implementation machine">{(slug==='choose-a-target'?machines.filter(m=>m.id==='commodore-64'):machines).map(m=><a href={`${prototypeRoot}${slug}/${slug==='choose-a-target'?'':m.id+'/'}`} aria-current={machine===m.id?'page':undefined}>{m.name}<span>{m.cpu}</span></a>)}</nav>
  {slug!=='choose-a-target'&&<nav class="connected-code" aria-label="Connected routines">{resolved.slice(0,3).map(t=><a href={`${prototypeRoot}${t.slug}/${machine}/`} aria-current={slug===t.slug?'page':undefined}>{t.title}</a>)}</nav>}
  <TechniqueCode slug={slug!} machine={machine}/>
  <details class="supporting-experiment"><summary>Understand the technique and its assumptions</summary>  <div class="technique-explanation"><p>{technique.explanation}</p><h2>What the pieces agree on</h2><p>{technique.contract}</p></div></details>
  <details class="supporting-experiment"><summary>{slug==='choose-a-target'?'Supporting experiment: inspect the target and next turn':'Supporting experiment: trace the input and sound events'}</summary><TechniqueExperiment kind={technique.experiment}/></details>
  <section class="implementation-section" aria-labelledby="implementations-title"><h2 id="implementations-title">{technique.implementations.length?'Choose an implementation':'The implementation in this prototype'}</h2>
   {technique.implementations.length?<><p>These existing entries provide the wider explanation and their original verification records. The reusable code and caller above are the starting point for this prototype.</p><ul class="implementation-list">{technique.implementations.map(i=><li><div><h3><a href={`/patterns/${i.id}/`}>{i.entry.data.title}</a></h3><p>{machineNames[i.entry.data.platform]} · {i.language}</p></div><div><strong>{i.evidence==='emulator-executed'?'Assembled and emulator-executed':'Illustrative listing'}</strong><p>{i.note}</p><p><a href={`/patterns/${i.id}/`}>Read the code, contract and evidence</a></p></div></li>)}</ul></>:<><p>The C64 routines above implement the original board’s target and local-turn choices. The optional browser board makes the same decisions visible. Other machine versions are not included in this first prototype.</p><p><a href="/vault/studies/pac-man/">The Pac-Man study</a> explores the historical rules, their quirks and the game around them. This experiment deliberately simplifies them.</p></>}
  </section>
  <section class="technique-explanation"><h2>Fit it into a game</h2><p>{technique.slug==='read-input'?'Read once at an agreed point in the update. Store the sample so movement and press detection see the same controls. Write an explicit adapter if the reader returns a different format.':technique.slug==='detect-presses'?'Use the stored current sample for continuous actions and the new-press result for single actions. Do not advance the history separately in each consumer.':technique.slug==='trigger-sound'?'Let the game emit an event, then hand it to the chosen driver. Read its stopping and ownership contract before connecting it to a running game.':'Keep target selection separate from legal movement. A new target rule can reuse the same turn chooser, but obstacles may demand a route-finding algorithm instead.'}</p>
   {technique.next.length>0&&<><h3>A useful next job</h3><ul>{technique.next.map(slug=>{const next=techniques.find(t=>t.slug===slug)!;return <li><a href={`${prototypeRoot}${slug}/${machine}/`}>{next.title}</a> — {next.task}</li>;})}</ul></>}
   {technique.slug==='trigger-sound'&&<p>For a wider comparison, see <a href="/patterns/cross-platform/audio/making-a-sound/">Four Ways to Make a Sound</a> and the <a href="/patterns/cross-platform/audio/tune-sequencer/">tune sequencer</a>. These are onward reading, not additional completed prototype implementations.</p>}
  </section>
  <footer class="pattern-next"><p><a href={prototypeRoot}>All four techniques</a> · <a href="/patterns/">Current Pattern Library</a></p></footer>
 </>}
 </div>
</Layout>
